     "answer": "No — nothing in the site records mentions a tower crane collapse; the crane records are about lifts, methodology and safety controls. [1][2]\n\n- 3 September: crane methodology and purlin deliveries discussed; decision that the subcontractor crane must lift from the bottom, and a medium safety note that the operator can't see the landing zone. [1]\n- 11 August: panel delivery delayed and wind picking up before the crane was demobbed; blockwork subbies caught walking under a suspended load, exclusion zones tightened. [2]\n- 10 September: CAA crane height limits requiring 10pm–6am lifts noted at governance. [3]\n- A search for any crane collapse or incident returned no matching records.",
     "final_raw": "No — nothing in the site records mentions a tower crane collapse; the crane records are about lifts, methodology and safety controls. [R5, R7]\n\n- 3 September: crane methodology and purlin deliveries discussed; decision that the subcontractor crane must lift from the bottom, and a medium safety note that the operator can't see the landing zone. [R5]\n- 11 August: panel delivery delayed and wind picking up before the crane was demobbed; blockwork subbies caught walking under a suspended load, exclusion zones tightened. [R7]\n- 10 September: CAA crane height limits requiring 10pm–6am lifts noted at governance. [R4]\n- A search for any crane collapse or incident returned no matching records.",
